WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.100
Do two molecules physically bind?

2
00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.500
Interactions were studied by cross-linking and by co-fractionation on

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.720
chromatography columns.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:17.320
A protein could be essential, yet have no known chemistry.

5
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.060
Yeast Ste5 never put a phosphate on anything or took one off.

6
00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.680
So what did it do?

7
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.040
Which parts do the binding?

8
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.540
And for a protein and DNA, at which sequence does it bind?

9
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:37.043
Immunoprecipitation starts with an extract that is not heated.

10
00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:43.703
An antibody fixed to protein A or G beads pulls its protein out of the mixture.

11
00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:47.303
Partners bound to that protein come out with it.

12
00:00:47.823 --> 00:00:52.943
They may be bound directly, bound indirectly, or held in a larger complex.

13
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:56.047
A pull-down uses a tag.

14
00:00:56.867 --> 00:01:02.547
Fuse glutathione S-transferase, called GST, to the protein, and run it over

15
00:01:02.547 --> 00:01:03.687
glutathione resin.

16
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.127
Only the tagged protein sticks.

17
00:01:06.887 --> 00:01:09.407
Add a lysate, and its partners can stick too.

18
00:01:10.187 --> 00:01:13.707
The tagged protein, called the bait, can be an isolated domain.

19
00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:17.487
Complexes are detected, for example, by western blot.

20
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.550
A gel shift handles protein and DNA.

21
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.170
Mix the protein with labelled DNA, and run it on a native gel.

22
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.110
Protein and DNA complexes generally migrate more slowly than free DNA.

23
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:37.070
Bound and free DNA separate, so both can be measured in one lane.

24
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:44.000
The catch from an immunoprecipitation is run on a gel, and blotted for a suspected

25
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.560
partner. A band there shows the two were in one complex.

26
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:56.440
On a gel shift, a slower band reports a DNA-binding factor, and how much of it is active.

27
00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.083
Binding in a lysate has limits.

28
00:02:00.603 --> 00:02:03.603
Co-immunoprecipitation does not say direct or indirect.

29
00:02:04.803 --> 00:02:09.663
Lysis removes compartments, so proteins that never meet in a cell can mix.

30
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:11.883
And resin can be sticky.

31
00:02:12.843 --> 00:02:18.303
In one study, antibodies to eight potassium channels all brought down syntaxin,

32
00:02:18.623 --> 00:02:20.003
which bound the resin itself.

33
00:02:21.603 --> 00:02:25.603
Control IPs with other antibodies, and mock IPs, exposed it.

34
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:30.223
In a pull-down, GST alone can serve as the control.

35
00:02:31.423 --> 00:02:33.863
Direct binding is tested with purified parts.

36
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:42.477
In 1990, Anderson, Pawson and colleagues made SH2 domains alone in bacteria.

37
00:02:43.517 --> 00:02:48.457
These domains bound growth factor receptors in lysates, only after stimulation.

38
00:02:49.297 --> 00:02:51.217
A domain on its own could be tested.

39
00:02:52.377 --> 00:02:59.857
In 1993, Oltvai, Milliman and Korsmeyer found a 21-kilodalton protein associated

40
00:02:59.857 --> 00:03:07.717
with BCL-2. They named it Bax, and reported that Bax forms heterodimers with BCL-2 in vivo.

41
00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.530
A gel shift showed a factor already waiting.

42
00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:19.770
In pre-B cells, lipopolysaccharide, or LPS, made NF-κB DNA-binding activity

43
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.570
appear, even with protein synthesis blocked.

44
00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:25.390
So the factor had to exist already.

45
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:31.310
Sen and Baltimore, 1986, called it a posttranslational activation.

46
00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:38.490
Keep this rule: binding in a lysate may be indirect, or happen only after lysis.
